/		SOURCE FILE CONFORMED TO OFFICIAL PDP-8 SOURCE CODE CONVENTIONS, AS
/		MANDATED BY P?S SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT POLICIES AND PROCEDURES DICTATED
/		PERSONALLY BY MARIO DENOBILI CIRCA 1968.

/		NOTE: MEMBERS OF THE WILLIAM CLAUDE FIELDS MEMORIAL PROGRAMMING GROUP
/		(WCFMPG) ARE KNOWN TO USE THE "\" CHARACTER WHERE MOST CONTEMPORANEOUS
/		COMPUTER PROFESSIONALS WOULD EMPLOY THE "/" CHARACTER.  IT IS THEORIZED
/		THIS VARIANCE LIKELY EVOLVED FROM THE MEMBERS' PERSONAL HISTORY WITH IBM
/		PRODUCTS AND RELATED OPERATING SYSTEM CONVENTIONS.

/		MEMBERS OF THE POLYTECHNIC QUESTION SOCIETY (P?S) GENERALLY DO NOT
/		SUPPORT THIS EARLIER CONVENTION.  AS SUCH, THE REST OF THIS SOURCE CODE
/		FILE WILL REFER TO PDP-8 MODELS AND OTHER DEFINITIONS USING THE "/"
/		CHARACTER, AS IS CUSTOMARY THROUGHOUT PDP-8 SOFTWARE.

/		LAST EDIT:	12-MAR-2013	CJL

/		THIS SOURCE CODE FILE IS A COMBINATION OF LAB-8/E BASIC AND 8K BASIC FOR
/		THE PDP-8.  TO CREATE THE LAB-8/E EDITION, SET "MACHINE=1"; TO CREATE
/		THE 8K PDP-8 EDITION, SET "MACHINE=0". [NOTE: 8K BASIC FOR THE PDP-8 IS
/		THE DEFAULT ASSEMBLY OPTION OF ALL RELEVANT GLOBAL ASSEMBLY PARAMETERS.]

/		THIS IS THE ORIGINAL 8K BASIC [AND LAB-8/E BASIC] DESIGNED FOR USE WITH
/		PAPER-TAPE SOFTWARE LOADERS [RIM AND BIN] ONLY.  SQUEEZING EVERY LAST
/		BIT OF AVAILABLE MEMORY SPACE WAS A DESIGN GOAL.  AS A RESULT, THERE ARE
/		DIFFICULTIES ASSOCIATED WITH LOADING FROM PREVAILING PDP-8 STORAGE-BASED
/		OPERATING SYSTEMS, ALL OF WHICH REQUIRE A SOMEWHAT LARGER RESERVED AREA
/		THAN THE PREVAILING PAPER-TAPE LOADERS.
/		BINARY PAPER-TAPE LOADING CONSIDERATIONS.

/		WHEN THE PAPER-TAPE BINARY LOADERS ARE IN EFFECT, THE HIGHEST LOCATION
/		IN ANY 4K MEMORY FIELD IS RESERVED AS A JUMP TO THE ACTUAL STARTING
/		ADDRESS OF THE BIN LOADER IN USE IN THAT FIELD.  THUS, THE OFFICIAL
/		STARTING ADDRESS OF ANY VERSION OF THE BIN LOADER IS 7777 OF THE FIELD
/		THE BIN LOADER IS OPERATING IN, ETC. [NOTE: AT LEAST THREE DIFFERENT
/		VERSIONS OF THE BIN LOADER HAVE BEEN RELEASED BY DIGITAL EQUIPMENT
/		CORPORATION; USER-WRITTEN VERSIONS ACCOUNT FOR SEVERAL MORE.]

/		IN THE SAME FIELD AS THE BIN LOADER, LOCATIONS 7756-7776 ARE RESERVED
/		FOR THE RIM LOADER.  SHOULD THE BIN LOADER BE DESTROYED, THE RIM LOADER
/		CAN BE USED TO RELOAD THE BIN LOADER TO IN TURN ALLOW LARGER PROGRAMS TO
/		BE LOADED BY THE BIN LOADER, ETC. [GENERALLY ONLY THE BIN LOADER IS READ
/		IN BY THE RIM LOADER.  RIM FORMAT IS BOTH LESS EFFICIENT AND LESS
/		RELIABLE THAN BIN FORMAT; HOWEVER THE RIM LOADER IS FAR SMALLER IN TERMS
/		OF CODE SPACE.]

/		THE AGGREGATE MEMORY RESERVATION FOR THE RIM AND BIN LOADERS IS IN THE
/		RANGE OF 7621-7777 [THE LOWEST ADDRESS VARIES ACCORDING TO THE ACTUAL
/		VERSION OF BIN IN EFFECT] THUS ALLOWING MARGINAL LOADING INTO THE
/		ADDRESS RANGE 7600-7620 OR SO, ETC.

/		ALL STORAGE-BASED PDP-8 OPERATING SYSTEMS MUST RESERVE ALL OF 7600-7777
/		IN FIELD 0.  SOME OPERATING SYSTEMS HAVE ADDITIONAL REQUIREMENTS
/		RESERVING EXTENDED MEMORY LOCATIONS DEPENDING ON SPECIFIC DESIGN AND
/		IN SOME CASES CONFIGURATIONAL ISSUES BEYOND SOME MINIMUM RESERVATION.
/		TO BE COMPATIBLE WITH A STORAGE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM, A PROGRAM MUST
/		CONFORM TO THE MORE STRINGENT LOADING RANGE OF 00000-07577 ONLY AS WELL
/		AS FURTHER CONSIDERATIONS IN EXTENDED MEMORY DEPENDING ON SPECIFIC
/		REQUIREMENTS OF EACH SYSTEM.

/		AS RELEASED, 8K BASIC AND LAB-8/E BASIC REQUIRE ALL OF FIELD 1 AS WELL
/		EXCEPT FOR 17756-17776 ALLOWING ONLY THE RIM LOADER TO BE PRESERVED
/		ASSUMING IT IS LOADED INTO FIELD 1.  THIS WILL BE IN CONFLICT WITH
/		CERTAIN OPERATING SYSTEMS MAKING THEM UNSUITABLE FOR SUPPORTING THE
/		PROGRAM. [NOTE: IN SOME CASES, THIS MAY BE PARTIALLY REMEDIED.]

/		CERTAIN PARAMETERS HAVE BEEN ADDED TO THIS RELEASE OF 8K AND LAB-8/E
/		BASIC TO MODIFY THE LOADING REQUIREMENTS THAT MAY BE SUITABLE FOR
/		SPECIFIC PURPOSES.  PROCEED WITH EXTREME CAUTION WHEN ATTEMPTING TO
/		CHANGE ANY ASPECT OF THIS PROGRAM TO AVOID SOFTWARE BLOWUPS.
/		FIELD 0 MEMORY CONSIDERATIONS.

/		THIS PROGRAM LOADS INTO THE USUAL MAXIMUM SPACE IN FIELD 0; SUCH
/		BEHAVIOR DOES NOT CONFLICT WITH ANY OF THE USUAL PDP-8 OPERATING
/		SYSTEMS.  oTHER THAN A FEW SMALL HOLES, THE PROGRAM LOADS INTO THE
/		ENTIRE MEMORY AREA OF 00000-07577. [NOTE: THE EARLIER 4K BASIC [WHICH
/		THIS PROGRAM IS BASED ON] CANNOT BE LOADED BY ANY STORAGE-BASED
/		OPERATING SYSTENM AND WILL DESTROY PARTS OF THE BIN AND RIM LOADER SPACE
/		AS IT STARTS UP.] THUS, REGARDING FIELD 0 USAGE, THERE SHOULD BE NO
/		PROBLEM RUNNING 8K BASIC OR LAB-8/E BASIC UNDER THE DISK MONITOR SYSTEM,
/		P?S/8 OR OS/8, AS THERE IS NO COMMUNICATION BETWEEN LAB-8/E BASIC OR
/		8K BASIC AND THE OPERATING SYSTEM [WITHOUT A NUMBER OF SEVERE
/		MODIFICATIONS NOT DEFINED HERE]. [NOTE: A FEASIBILITY STUDY IS BEING
/		UNDERTAKEN AS TO THE BEST WAY TO INTERFACE LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC TO
/		P?S/8 IN TERMS OF FILE SUPPORT, ETC.  IT IS EXPECTED THAT A SEPARATE
/		OVERLAY FILE WILL BE USED TO ACCOMPLISH THIS RESULT, MUCH THE SAME WAY
/		AS FOCAL, 1969 WAS INTERFACED TO THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM AND THE LATER
/		P?S/8.  BINARY COMPATIBILITY WILL BE AN IMPORTANT GOAL.]

/		GENERAL EXTENDED MEMORY CONSIDERATIONS.

/		ONE NEARLY COMPLETE MEMORY FIELD IS ALSO NEEDED, GENERALLY FIELD 1.
/		BECAUSE OF ORIGINAL DESIGN SPECIFICATIONS ALLOWING ONLY FOR THE RIM
/		LOADER IN FIELD 1, IT IS IMPOSSIBLE TO RUN LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC
/		FROM OS/8 WITHOUT DESTROYING PART OF THE RESIDENT OPERATING SYSTEM.
/		MOST, BUT NOT ALL, P?S/8 SYSTEMS CAN RUN LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC AS
/		LONG AS THERE IS SUFFICIENT FREE MEMORY IN FIELD 1 [OR ANY OTHER
/		NON-CONFLICTING MEMORY FIELD, IF LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K-BASIC IS
/		REASSEMBLED WITH NON-STANDARD SETTINGS].

/		OS/8 EXTENDED MEMORY CONSIDERATIONS.

/		PROVISIONAL AND HIGHLY RESTRICTED USAGE UNDER OS/8 REQUIRES 12K MEMORY
/		MINIMUM TO AVOID CONFLICT WITH THE OS/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AND OTHER
/		OS/8 SYSTEM CONVENTIONS IN EFFECT REGARDING RESERVED MEMORY LOCATIONS IN
/		FIELDS 0 AND 1.  ADDITIONALLY, THIS ASSUMES SCRUPULOUS AVOIDANCE OF OS/8
/		BATCH AT THE USER'S PERIL.  THIS REQUIREMENT RISES TO 16K IF 1) OS/8 BATCH
/		IS CONTEMPLATED, OR 2) THE OS/8 SYSTEM HANDLER IS WRITTEN TO REQUIRE
/		[NEARLY] TWO PAGES WHERE THE SECOND PAGE OF MEMORY USED IS DEFINED AS
/		27600-27774 THUS REQUIRING 12K MINIMUM.  [ASSUMING 8K BASIC OR LAB-8/E
/		BASIC WERE CUSTOMIZED TO REQUIRE 12K, LOCATIONS 27600-27755 WOULD BE IN
/		CONFLICT WITH THE REQUIREMENTS OF ANY OS/8 TWO-PAGE SYSTEM DEVICE
/		HANDLER AND/OR OS/8 BATCH WHICH ALSO REQUIRES A PORTION OF FIELD 2.]
/		[NOTE: OS/8 TWO-PAGE SYSTEM HANDLERS MAY BE RELOCATED TO HIGHER MEMORY
/		FIELDS BY OTHER APPLICATIONS!  THIS IS SUFFICIENT REASON WHY OS/8 CAN
/		NEVER BE TOTALLY COMPATIBLE WITH 8K BASIC OR LAB-8/E BASIC.  SIMILAR
/		REASONING APPLIES INDEPENDENTLY TO OS/8 BATCH WHICH MAY ALSO BE
/		RELOCATED TO LOCATIONS ADJACENT TO THE TWO-PAGE HANDLER RESERVED AREA
/		REGARDLESS OF SYSTEM HANDLER TYPE, ETC.]
/		EVEN WITH THE EXTRA CONSIDERATIONS MET, THE USE OF LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K
/		BASIC IS STILL NOT "LEGAL" UNDER OS/8 BECAUSE IT VIOLATES THE OS/8
/		STANDARD OF RESERVING MEMORY AREA X7600-X7777 FOR EVERY MEMORY FIELD X.
/		CERTAIN SPECIFIC CONFIGURATIONS OF OS/8 SYSTEMS MAY CRASH DESPITE
/		POSSIBLY ACCEPTABLE USE ON SOME OTHER SYSTEMS; THIS IS BECAUSE OS/8 WAS
/		NEVER DESIGNED TO HANDLE PROGRAMS THAT UTILIZE MEMORY AS FREELY AS LAB-8/E
/		BASIC OR 8K BASIC WHICH PUSH THE UPPER ENVELOPE OF PAPER-TAPE LOADING
/		CONVENTIONS.

/		ADDITIONALLY, OS/8 WILL LIKELY NOT BE ABLE TO DEBUG LAB-8/E BASIC
/		PROGRAM CODE IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD [WHICH MUST BE FIELD 2 OR,
/		MORE LIKELY, FIELD 3 OR HIGHER] DUE TO INTERACTION WITH THE EXTENDED
/		MEMORY BREAKPOINT CODE OF OS/8 ODT.  [NOTE: ODT ASSOCIATED WITH CERTAIN
/		VERSIONS OF THE OS/8 FAMILY WILL LIKELY ATTEMPT TO PLACE BREAKPOINT CODE
/		INTO EVERY MEMORY FIELD IN WHICH A BREAKPOINT *MIGHT* BE PRESENT.  AS
/		SUCH, ODT MAY NOT BE VIABLE AT ALL WHILE ATTEMPTING SUCH “QUASI-POSSIBLE”
/		OS/8 USE. [8K BASIC DOES NOT HAVE ANY EXTENDED MEMORY PROGRAM CODE;
/		SOFTWARE DEBUGGERS CANNOT TRAP DATA ACCESS OPERATIONS.]

/		TO ATTEMPT TO ACCOMPLISH THIS, A NON-STANDARD BINARY VERSION MUST BE
/		CREATED USING THE SETTING FOR THE EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD AS 2 OR, MORE
/		LIKELY, 3 OR HIGHER.  THE DEFAULT IS SET TO FIELD 1 TO ALLOW THE
/		ORIGINAL VERSION TO RUN ON 8K MACHINES WITHOUT OS/8 PRESENT.  SEE BELOW
/		TO CHANGE THE BINARY OUTPUT CONFIGURATION FOR TENTATIVE PROVISIONAL USE
/		WITH OS/8.  NOTE:  UNLESS THE CAPABILITIES OF LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC ARE
/		GREATLY REDUCED, UNCONDITIONAL USAGE UNDER OS/8 IS NOT POSSIBLE.

/		P?S/8 EXTENDED MEMORY CONFIGURATION CONSIDERATIONS.

/		P?S/8 GENERALLY WILL SUPPORT LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC ON 8K OR LARGER
/		SYSTEMS UNLESS THE CONFIGURATION FALLS INTO A LIMITED CLASS OF SYSTEMS:
/		1) EXACTLY 8K PHYSICAL MEMORY, AND 2) THE REQUIREMENT OF VARIOUS
/		MANDATORY AND/OR OPTIONAL COMPONENTS PRECLUDES COMPLETE AVAILABILITY OF
/		FIELD 1.

/		P?S/8 EXTENDED MEMORY LAYOUT ISSUES.

/		P?S/8 CONFIGURATIONS OF 12K OR MORE CAN RUN LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC
/		UNCONDITIONALLY. [4K CONFIGURATIONS CANNOT RUN LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC
/		AND THERE ARE NO PLANS TO CREATE A VIRTUAL/SWAPPING VERSION TO OVERCOME
/		THIS RESTRICTION.  IN SUCH AN IMPLEMENTATION THERE WOULD BE FAR TOO MUCH
/		OVERHEAD OF THRASHING ASSOCIATED WITH ANY FORM OF CODE STACK OR OTHER
/		VIRTUALIZED DATA MANAGEMENT.]

/		CERTAIN P?S/8 CONFIGURATIONS MAY INCLUDE MODULES THAT LOAD INTO THE
/		HIGHEST PHYSICAL FIELD OF MEMORY [FIELD "X", AS IS USED IN THE FOLLOWING
/		DESCRIPTION OF THE RELEVANT COMPONENTS].

/		8K P?S/8 CONFIGURATIONS [WHERE "X" WOULD BE DYNAMICALLY CONFIGURED AS 1]
/		CAN ONLY RUN LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC IF ALL EXTENDED MEMORY COMPONENTS
/		OF P?S/8 ARE NOT PRESENT OR DISABLED:
/		X0000-X5377 - LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY.  THIS FEATURE CAN BE DISABLED BY
/			      THE USER.  THE CONSOLE, LINEPRINTER AND INTERRUPT
/			      FUNCTIONS OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY CANNOT BE
/			      ACCESSED BY THIS VERSION OF LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC.
/			      THE ORIGINAL DESIGN IS STRICTLY FOR STANDALONE USAGE
/			      WITHOUT A STORAGE DEVICE-BASED OPERATING SYSTEM.

/			      MOST OF THE FEATURES OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY DO NOT
/			      PERTAIN TO THE STANDALONE VERSION OF LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K
/			      BASIC.  ONLY THE SYSTEM DEVICE-ORIENTED COMPONENTS OF THE
/			      LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY ARE RELEVANT.

/		X5400-X5777 - SHELL SYSTEM COMMUNICATIONS AREA.  USAGE OF THIS PORTION
/			      OF MEMORY CAN BE AVOIDED BY THE USER BY ONE OF TWO
/			      METHODS: 1) DISABLING THE P?S/8 SHELL, OR 2) RUNNING THE
/			      P?S/8 SHELL WITH THE SHELL SYSTEM COMMUNICATION AREA
/			      VIRTUALIZED TO THE CONTENTS OF TWO [LOW-NUMBERED] SYSTEM
/			      BLOCKS.

/			      VIRTUALIZING THE SHELL SYSTEM COMMUNICATIONS AREA WILL
/			      GENERALLY CAUSES A SMALL PERFORMANCE LOSS DURING CERTAIN
/			      OPERATIONS THAT REPEATEDLY ACCESS THE TWO BLOCKS.  [NOTE:
/			      P?S/8 BLOCKS ARE 128 WORDS EACH; OS/8 LOGICAL RECORDS ARE
/			      256 WORDS EACH.] FOR SYSTEMS WITH LESS THAN 12K, THE
/			      VIRTUALIZATION METHOD IS GENERALLY AN ACCEPTABLE
/			      COMPROMISE BETWEEN PERFORMANCE AND MEMORY AVAILABILITY.

/			      [NOTE: THE P?S/8 SHELL IS DESIGNED FOR LARGER MEMORY
/			      CONFIGURATIONS THAT CAN BETTER MAKE USE OF THE ADVANCED
/			      SHELL FEATURES, ETC.  ON 4K SYSTEMS THE VIRTUALIZATION
/			      IS MANDATORY AND ALL EXTENDED MEMORY COMPONENTS MUST BE
/			      DISABLED.  MOST SHELL COMPONENTS WILL ISSUE ERROR MESSAGES
/			      ON INADEQUATE MEMORY CONFIGURATIONS.]

/		X6000-X7777 - SYSTEM HANDLER EXTENSION.  THIS IS A MANDATORY COMPONENT
/			      OF ALL P?S/8 SYSTEMS THAT REQUIRE EXTENDED MEMORY TO
/			      OPERATE DUE TO SPECIFIC HARDWARE ISSUES.  AS SUCH, SYSTEMS
/			      THAT INCLUDE THE SYSTEM HANDLER EXTENSION CANNOT RUN
/			      LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC UNLESS THERE IS 12K OR MORE
/			      PHYSICAL MEMORY PRESENT [WHICH WOULD DYNAMICALLY MAKE X=2
/			      OR MORE, X=7 ON A 32K MACHINE].

/			      KNOWN HARDWARE CONFIGURATIONS THAT CANNOT RUN ON A 4K
/			      MACHINE INCLUDE THE TD8E/TU56[H] DECTAPE WHEN USED WITHOUT
/			      THE MR8E-C ROM, RX01/RX02 AND THIRD-PARTY COMPATIBLES
/			      [DSD-210], UNMODIFIED LINC-8 [WHICH REQUIRES AT LEAST 8K
/			      TO OPERATE P?S/8; 4K LINC-8 SYSTEMS REQUIRE DESIGNATED
/			      HARDWARE MODIFICATIONS.  OS/8 CANNOT RUN ON AN UNMODIFIED
/			      8K LINC-8; IT CAN RUN ON A MODIFIED 8K LINC-8],
/			      RX50/DECMATE II, III AND III+ SYSTEMS, RK01/RK08,
/			      RK8E/RK05, RL8A/RL01, RL8A/RL02 AND CESI MDC8 SCSI
/			      CONTROLLER WITH 256 WORD DISK BLOCKS.
/			      THE MDC8 SUPPORTS TWO DIFFERENT DISK FORMATS: 256 BYTE AND
/			      512 BYTE DISK SECTORS THAT YIELD 128 WORD AND 256 WORD
/			      RECORDS, RESPECTIVELY.  ON 128 WORD DISKS, P?S/8 AND OS/8
/			      CAN RUN ON 4K AND 8K MINIMUM SYSTEMS, RESPECTIVELY.  P?S/8
/			      REQUIRES A SYSTEM HANDLER EXTENSION TO RUN ON THE 256 WORD
/			      VERSION IN 8K MINIMUM; OS/8 CANNOT BE RUN ON THIS SYSTEM.

/			      THE 256 WORD VERSION IS USUALLY FOUND ON PDP-8/A MACHINES
/			      CONFIGURED WITH CESI EXTENDED MEMORY CONFIGURATIONS THAT
/			      ARE NOT COMPATIBLE WITH THE KT8A.  HOWEVER, THEY CAN BE
/			      CONFIGURED FOR UP TO 512K MEMORY INSTEAD OF THE KT8A
/			      MAXIMUM OF 128K.

/			      THE DESIGNATED SOFTWARE APPLICATION FOR THESE
/			      CONFIGURATIONS IS OMNI-8, A STANDALONE APPLICATION SYSTEM
/			      THAT EMULATES MULTIPLE PDP-8 SYSTEMS VIRTUALLY USING THE
/			      MDC8 FOR BACKING STORE.  TO MINIMIZE DISK THRASHING, EACH
/			      USER IMAGE IS MAINTAINED IN 32K OF THE AVAILABLE MEMORY.
/			      THIS ALLOWS UP TO 15 USERS TO SHARE THE UNDERLYING SYSTEM
/			      WITHOUT DISK SWAPPING, ALTHOUGH PRACTICAL SYSTEMS WERE
/			      OFTEN CONFIGURED FOR A SOMEWHAT HIGHER NUMBER OF OVERALL
/			      USERS, EACH RUNNING A VIRTUAL MACHINE.

/			      THE VIRTUAL MACHINES CAN SUPPORT A VARIETY OF OPERATING
/			      SYSTEMS INCLUDING AN OS/8 SYSTEM USING TRAP EMULATION
/			      HANDLERS NOT CORRESPONDING TO ANY NORMAL PHYSICAL DEVICE.
/			      ALL OMNI-8 FUNCTIONS MUST BE RUN FROM THE OMNI-8
/			      APPLICATION SYSTEM OR FROM P?S/8 CONFIGURED TO RUN AND
/			      SUPPORT THE SAME UNDERLYING HARDWARE.  ATTEMPTS AT RUNNING
/			      8K BASIC OR LAB-8/E BASIC ON VIRTUALIZED HARDWARE WOULD BE
/			      SUBJECT TO SIMILAR RESTRICTIONS AS OUTLINED ELSEWHERE.]

/		THERE MAY BE ADDITIONAL P?S/8 CONFIGURATIONS REQUIRING MORE THAN 4K
/		WHERE OS/8 EITHER CANNOT RUN AT ALL OR WILL EXHIBIT UNACCEPTABLE ERROR
/		RECOVERY AND/OR PERFORMANCE ISSUES.

/		SINCE P?S/8 ALLOWS FOR A TOTAL OF [NEARLY] NINE PAGES OF CODE SPACE,
/		P?S/8 HANDLERS GENERALLY EXHIBIT EXEMPLARY PERFORMANCE AND PROPER ERROR
/		RECOVERY THAT SIMPLY CANNOT BE ACHIEVED IN [NEARLY] TWO PAGES OF CODE
/		SPACE, AS MUST BE THE CASE IN OS/8.

/		AS SUCH, IT WILL GENERALLY BE TRUE FOR THIS CLASS OF DEVICES THAT P?S/8
/		WILL REQUIRE A MINIMUM OF 8K TO OPERATE AND 12K TO RUN LAB-8/E BASIC OR
/		8K BASIC.  OS/8 WILL ALWAYS REQUIRE 12K OR MORE TO RUN, PROVISIONALLY,
/		LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC--ASSUMING THIS IS EVEN POSSIBLE. [SUCH
/		OPERATION WOULD NEVER BE RECOMMENDED UNLESS SOME STORAGE RESOURCES WERE
/		REMOVED FROM LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC BY MODIFYING THE SOURCE CODE.]
/		KNOWN LIMITATIONS.

/		THE LAB-8/E VERSION HAS A KNOWN BUG.  ON CERTAIN CONFIGURATIONS
/		CONTAINING MORE THAN THE MINIMUM COMPLEMENT OF LAB-8/E HARDWARE,
/		LOCATION 10000 AND PERHAPS THE FOLLOWING TWO WORDS MAY BE DESTROYED!
/		THESE LOCATIONS ARE NORMALLY USED TO CONTAIN THE FLOATING POINT CONSTANT
/		VALUE 10.0,USED TO CALCULATE LINE NUMBERS AS PART OF A LIST COMMAND
/		OPERATION [AS WELL AS A COMPONENT OF OTHER PROGRAM SECTIONS].  uNDER
/		THESE CIRCUMSTANCES, QUITE BIZARRE LINE NUMBERS MAY APPEAR, ETC.  THIS
/		ISSUE NEEDS TO BE ADDRESSED; IT IS HOPED THAT A SMALL PATCH THAT BETTER
/		CLEARS INTERRUPTS IS ALL THAT IS NEEDED.  HOWEVER, THE CLEAR ALL FLAGS
/		INSTRUCTION IS NOT SAFE FOR ALL CONFIGURATIONS AND GENERALLY MUST BE
/		AVOIDED.  THUS, SOME ONCE-ONLY CODE SHOULD BE ADDED THAT CHECKS FOR THE
/		PDP-8/E OR PDP-8/A [OR NEWER] CPU TO ALLOW THE SAFE ISSUANCE OF THE CAF
/		INSTRUCTION [6007] TO REMEDY THIS BEHAVIOR.  BY CAREFUL CODE PLACEMENT,
/		BINARY COMPATIBILITY CAN BE MAINTAINED ESSENTIALLY BY IMPLEMENTATION AS
/		A ONCE-ONLY PATCH.  AS OF THIS WRITING, THIS ISSUE HAS NOT BEEN
/		ADDRESSED, PENDING A PROPER TESTING ENVIRONMENT.

/		CORE COMMAND CONSIDERATIONS.

/		P?S/8 AND OS/8 BOTH SUPPORT A LOGICAL CORE SIZE PARAMETER AND COMMAND
/		SET TO LOGICALLY LIMIT CORE SIZE TO SMALLER VALUES THAN THE ACTUAL
/		PHYSICAL SIZE.

/		IT IS ASSUMED THE CORE SIZE IS NOT LIMITED TO A VALUE SMALLER THAN THE
/		REQUIREMENTS OF ANY ASSEMBLED VERSION OF THIS PROGRAM.  AS DOCUMENTED
/		EARLIER, OS/8 USAGE IS NOT ONLY PROVISIONAL, BUT DEPENDS ON HAVING
/		SUFFICIENT PHYSICAL MEMORY TO PROPERLY LOAD THE PROGRAM.  TOO LOWER A
/		LOGICAL CORE SIZE SETTING MAY INTERFERE WITH THIS SITUATION PREVENTING
/		A WORKING PROGRAM OF ANY NATURE.

/		THE SAME GENERAL IDEA APPLIES TO P?S/8 WHICH TAKES THE CONCEPT ONE STEP
/		FURTHER.  WHILE OS/8 CAN LIMIT THE CORE SIZE TO 8K [THE OS/8 MINIMUM],
/		P?S/8 CAN LIMIT THE CORE SIZE TO 4K, A VALUE THAT CANNOT SUPPORT ANY
/		VERSION OF THIS PROGRAM. USING SUFFICIENTLY LARGE CORE SIZE VALUES WILL
/		ALLOW PROPER LOADING UNDER EACH OPERATING SYSTEM, ETC.

/		SETTING THE EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD.

/		THE DEFAULT SETTING OF "XFLD=1" WILL PRODUCE THE STANDARD VERSION OF
/		LAB-8/E OR 8K BASIC AS ORIGINALLY DISTRIBUTED.  THIS VERSION CAN BE
/		LOADED ON 8K MACHINES USING THE BINARY PAPER-TAPE LOADERS OR UNDER THE
/		DISK MONITOR SYSTEM OR P?S/8 WITHOUT FIELD 1 RESTRICTIONS OR ANY P?S/8
/		SYSTEM RUNNING ON A 12K OR LARGER MACHINE.  SETTING "XFLD=2" OR "XFLD=3"
/		WILL PRODUCE A VERSION THAT CAN BE PROVISIONALLY RUN FROM OS/8.  LARGER
/		VALUES ARE ALSO ACCEPTABLE IF THE PHYSICAL MEMORY IS PRESENT.
/		DEBUGGING ISSUES.

/		AS WRITTEN, LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC DOES NOT CONFORM TO PDP-8
/		SOFTWARE DEBUGGING CONVENTIONS AS LOCATION 00004 IS USED BY THE PROGRAM.
/		ALL PDP-8 DEBUGGING PROGRAMS REQUIRE BREAKPOINT IMPLEMENTATION USING
/		LOCATION 00004 ASSUMING FIELD 0 CODE IS BEING DEBUGGED.  THE BREAKPOINT
/		MECHANISM FOR OS/8 IS JMP I 4 TO TRAP TO OS/8 ODT DURING A DEBUGGING
/		SESSION.  P?S/8 IS SIMILAR EXCEPT P?S/8 ODT USES JMS I 4 TO SUPPORT
/		MULTIPLE BREAKPOINTS.  BY STORING THE COMPLETE BREAKPOINT TABLE, P?S/8
/		ODT CAN PROVIDE AN ADDITIONAL LEVEL OF SAFETY AND RELIABILITY.

/		BREAKPOINT SPOOFING WARNINGS.

/		WHEN PROGRAMS HAVE MARGINAL SPACE TO CONFORM WITH SOFTWARE DEBUGGERS,
/		PROGRAMMERS MIGHT BE TEMPTED TO SPOOF OR FAKE BREAKPOINTS SUCH AS CAN BE
/		ATTEMPTED IN PAPER-TAPE VERSIONS OF ODT OR PERHAPS OS/8 ODT.

/		IN P?S/8, BREAKPOINTS ARE REQUIRED TO BE LEGITIMATE AS A VALIDATED TABLE
/		OF MULTIPLE BREAKPOINT ADDRESSES IS MAINTAINED.  ILLEGAL BREAKPOINTS
/		WILL BE FLAGGED AS ERRORS TO SAFEGUARD THE DEBUGGING ENVIRONMENT.
/		HOWEVER, IN STAND-ALONE OR OS/8 ODT, IT IS MUCH EASIER TO FAKE A
/		BREAKPOINT BY CAUSING A PROGRAM TO GET TO THE SAME PLACE AS THE PROPER
/		JMP I 4 WAS DESIGNED TO ACCOMPLISH; THE DEBUGGER CANNOT REALIZE IT IS
/		BEING SPOOFED WHETHER ON PURPOSE OR ACCIDENTALLY.  THUS, IT IS QUITE
/		DANGEROUS TO CONSIDER SUCH MECHANISMS WHICH CAN FATALLY DESTROY PROPER
/		PROGRAM FLOW AND ALTHOUGH UNLIKELY, COULD IN THEORY DESTROY THE
/		OPERATING SYSTEM, PERHAPS ON THE STORAGE DEVICE, ETC.  SINGLE-BREAKPOINT
/		MECHANISMS CANNOT HELP BUT TO SIMULATE THE APPARENT TRAP LOCATION AND
/		THUS CONTINUE BY PRESUMING THIS IS THE PROPER COURSE OF ACTION.  P?S/8
/		CAN CONTINUE FROM AN ILLEGAL BREAKPOINT, BUT ONLY AFTER A WARNING
/		MESSAGE HAD BEEN ISSUED AND TOTALLY AT THE USER'S POTENTIAL PERIL;
/		THIS IS NEVER RECOMMENDED.

/		TO AVOID LEGITIMATE DEBUGGING CONFLICTS, LOCATION 00004 WILL BE FREED IF
/		POSSIBLE.  THIS CAN ONLY BE ACCOMPLISHED IF THERE IS A FREE WORD ON PAGE
/		ZERO, WHICH IS PRESUMED AT THIS TIME.  THIS IS NOT KNOWN WITH CERTAINTY
/		AT THE TIME OF THIS WRITING AND CAUTION SHOULD BE USED ENABLING
/		PARAMETERS IN FUTURE RELEASES OF THIS PROGRAM UNTIL THIS FEATURE [NOT
/		CURRENTLY AVAILABLE] IS FULLY TESTED.

/		EXTENDED MEMORY DEBUGGING CONSIDERATIONS.

/		EACH FIELD IN WHICH A BREAKPOINT IS INSERTED REQUIRES FREEING LOCATION
/		0004 OF THAT FIELD.  THE STANDARD VERSION OF 8K BASIC HAS NO EXECUTABLE
/		CODE IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD.  HOWEVER, THE LAB-8/E VERSION DOES
/		REQUIRE SEVERAL PAGES OF CODE IN EXTENDED MEMORY.  TO FACILITATE
/		BREAKPOINT PLACEMENT IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD, A SECONDARY
/		PARAMETER IS PROVIDED TO PROTECT A FEW LOCATIONS IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY
/		FIELD, SHOULD THIS BECOME NECESSARY.  HOWEVER, EXTENDED MEMORY DEBUGGING
/		IN ALL RELEVANT ENVIRONMENTS MUST OF NECESSITY ALSO RESERVE PART OF
/		7600-7777 IN THAT FIELD.  AS SUCH, ONLY A VERSION ASSEMBLED TO
/		CONSIDERABLY FORESHORTEN THE VARIABLE SPACE IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD
/		CAN EVEN ATTEMPT THIS.  [SEE ALSO INTERRUPT CONSIDERATIONS AS
/		DISCUSSED BELOW.]
/		NOTES: CHANGING ANY OF THE DEFAULT PARAMETERS DESTROYS SOME ASPECT OF
/		BINARY COMPATIBILITY WITH THE ORIGINAL 8K BASIC OR LAB-8/E BASIC.  WHEN
/		EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD DEBUGGING IS ENABLED, THE MAXIMUM SIZE OF BASIC
/		CODE MUST BE LIMITED TO FACILITATE THE DEBUGGING SESSION, ETC.  AS SUCH,
/		DYNAMIC DATA STORAGE LOCATIONS MUST BE FORESHORTENED TO ADDRESSES NOT
/		COMPATIBLE WITH STANDARD LISTINGS OF THIS PROGRAM.  THUS, AN ADDITIONAL
/		CONSIDERATION WHEN PERFORMING EXTENDED MEMORY DEBUGGING OF THE LAB-8/E
/		FUNCTION CODE WOULD BE TO CREATE A CORRECT LISTING CORRESPONDING TO THE
/		DEBUGGING ENVIRONMENT REQUIREMENTS.

/		INTERRUPT CONSIDERATIONS DURING DEBUGGING.

/		LAB-8/E BASIC AND 8K-BASIC RUN WITH INTERRUPTS ENABLED.  THIS LIMITS
/		DEBUGGING TO SECTIONS OF CODE WITH INTERRUPTS OFF.  IT MAY BE NECESSARY
/		TO CONTRIVE ACCEPTABLE BREAKPOINTS BY APPLYING LOCAL PATCHES TO TURN
/		INTERRUPTS OFF AND LIMIT THE BREAKPOINTS TO THESE SAFELY PATCHED AREAS,
/		WHICH CONTAIN ADDITIONAL CODE TO TURN INTERRUPTS ON AGAIN AFTERWARDS.

/		AN INHERENT PROBLEM IS DETERMINING THE TIMING RELEVANCE OF PLACING ANY
/		PATCHES THAT TURN INTERRUPTS OFF AND THEN RESTORING THEM.  MAINTAINING
/		BINARY COMPATIBILITY WILL GENERALLY REQUIRE FUDGING IN A PAIR OF
/		SUBROUTINES TO REPLACE A PAIR OF INSTRUCTIONS WITH A JMP TO A FIXUP
/		ROUTINE ON ANOTHER PAGE THAT INCLUDES EITHER IOF OR ION AS APPROPRIATE,
/		FOLLOWED BY A JUMP BACK TO BEYOND WHERE THE TWO-WORD PATCHES WERE
/		ORIGINALLY LOCATED.  THERE ARE SUFFICIENT FREE CODE SPACE AREAS,
/		PRIMARILY IN THE 07400-07577 PAGE ASSUMING NO USER FUNCTIONS HAVE ALSO
/		BEEN LOADED, ETC.  EVEN IF DEBUGGED WITH P?S/8 ODT, IT IS DIFFICULT TO
/		CREATE MANY SUCH CONTRIVED INTERRUPTIBLE CODE SECTIONS; DEBUGGING WILL
/		GENERALLY HAVE TO BE LIMITED TO A CODE TOTALLY CONTAINED WITHIN A
/		REGION OF CODE SPACE LOCATED INSIDE OF BRACKETING INTERRUPT CONTROL
/		PATCHES, ETC.

/		IT WILL LIKELY BE NECESSARY TO RESERVE ADDITIONAL SPACE FOR THESE
/		PATCHES WHEN CONSIDERING EXTENDED MEMORY DEBUGGING.  THE MAXIMUM NUMBER
/		OF PATCHES TO BE APPLIED WILL FACTOR INTO THIS CONSIDERATIONS WHICH TO
/		SOME EXTENT FURTHER COMPLICATES EXTENDED MEMORY DEBUGGING, ETC.  AS
/		STATED ABOVE, IT WILL ESSENTIALLY BE MANDATORY TO HAVE A PROPER CURRENT
/		LISTING OF THE PROGRAM TO ALLOW PRAGMATIC CREATION OF BREAKPOINTS THAT
/		EFFECT EXTENDED MEMORY CODE DEBUGGING.
/		ASSEMBLY INSTRUCTIONS.

/		THIS PROGRAM MAKES HEAVY USE OF THE TEXT DIRECTIVE EXPECTING EVEN-LENGTH
/		STRINGS TO BE ZERO-TERMINATED.  THIS BEHAVIOR IS CONTROLLED BY THE USE
/		OF A COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCH WHEN INVOKING THE ASSEMBLER PROGRAM.

/		WHILE OTHER SWITCHES MAY APPLY [EACH ASSEMBLER USES DIFFERENT OPTION
/		SWITCHES FOR DIFFERENT PURPOSES], THE FOLLOWING SETTINGS ARE REQUIRED TO
/		PRODUCE A PROPER ASSEMBLY:

/		WHEN USING PAL8 TO ASSEMBLE THIS PROGRAM, THE /F SWITCH MUST NOT BE SET.
/		THIS WILL ENSURE THE REQUIRED TEXT CONVENTION IS CONFORMED TO.

/		WHEN USING P?S/8 PAL TO ASSEMBLE THIS PROGRAM, THE /J SWITCH MUST NOT BE
/		SET. THIS WILL ENSURE THE REQUIRED TEXT CONVENTION IS CONFORMED TO.

/		ALL LATTER-DAY RELEASES OF OS/8 AND P?S/8 INCLUDE ASSEMBLERS THAT FULLY
/		SUPPORT THE PAL LANGUAGE FEATURE SET AS NEEDED FOR SERIOUS PDP-8
/		PROGRAMMING.  [NOTE: THE OS/8 FORERUNNER SYSTEM, PS/8, IS GENERALLY NOT
/		COMPATIBLE WITH THIS REQUIREMENT AS IT USED A VERY EARLY VERSION OF
/		PAL8 THAT WAS INCOMPATIBLE IN MANY WAYS.]

/		IT IS NOT KNOWN WITH CERTAINTY WHAT OTHER PDP-8 ASSEMBLERS [OR POSSIBLY
/		CROSS-ASSEMBLERS] ARE COMPATIBLE WITH THIS SOURCE CODE.  IN THE CASE OF
/		TOPS-10 PAL10, FAILURE TO REMAIN CURRENT ON ADDED FEATURES HAS BECOME AN
/		IMPORTANT ISSUE.

/		DEPENDING ON CERTAIN OPERATIONS USED IN THIS ASSEMBLY, PAL10 IS PROBABLY
/		NOT A PROPER CANDIDATE ASSEMBLER FOR SEVERAL REASONS:

/		1) THE TEXT DIRECTIVE BEHAVIOR IN PAL10 IS ARBITRARY AND DOESN'T SUPPORT
/		   A COMMAND-LINE SWITCH TO REVERSE THE BEHAVIOR.  [NOTE: ON THIS POINT
/		   IT MIGHT FUNCTION ANYWAY, BUT IN THE GENERAL CASE WOULDN'T UNLESS THE
/		   BEHAVIOR CAN BE SET EITHER WAY AS IN THE OTHER ASSEMBLERS.]

/		2) THE ZBLOCK DIRECTIVE IS KNOWN TO BE DEFECTIVELY IMPLEMENTED.  ANY
/		   ATTEMPT TO CREATE 4000 OCTAL OR MORE ZEROS CAUSES AN ERRONEOUS ERROR.
/		   PROPER IMPLEMENTATION DEMANDS 7777 OCTAL ZEROES BE ALLOWED.

/		3) ATTEMPTS TO REDEFINE A SYMBOL WHEN THE REDEFINITION CHANGES THE
/		   VALUE USING A LABEL EXPRESSION IS AN ERROR IN ALL ASSEMBLERS.
/		   HOWEVER, IF THE VALUE REMAINS UNCHANGED, ONLY PAL10 CONSIDERS THIS
/		   AS AN ERROR; IT IS INTEGRAL TO MUCH WELL-ORGANIZED PDP-8 PROGRAMMING
/		   THAT THIS PROGRAMMING ELEMENT BE PROPERLY SUPPORTED SINCE OVERLAYS
/		   AND RELATED ORIGIN SETTING USAGES THAT REDEFINE SYMBOLS IDENTICALLY
/		   MUST BE SUPPORTED. [THE PROGRAMMER RELIES ON THE ASSEMBLER TO
/		   INDICATE CHANGED VALUES ONLY, NOT FALSELY REPORT REDUNDANT USAGE THAT
/		   MERELY CONFIRMS SECTIONS OF CODE ARE IN SYNC WITH EACH OTHER, ETC.]

/		4) SEVERAL SOMEWHAT NEWER FEATURES VITALLY NEEDED IN MORE MODERN PDP-8
/		   PROGRAMMING ARE NOT SUPPORTED IN PAL10 SUCH AS THE RELOC DIRECTIVE.

/		IT IS QUITE UNFORTUNATE THIS SHORT LIST OF FEATURES PREVENTS PAL10 FROM
/		BEING USED; A COMPETENT PDP-10 PROGRAMMER SHOULD BE ABLE TO REMEDY THESE
/		AND OTHER ISSUES TO MAKE PAL10 FULLY COMPATIBLE.
/	GLOBAL SETTINGS.

/	THESE SETTINGS ALLOW CUSTOM MODIFICATION TO FACILITATE SOME FORM OF DEBUGGING
/	OR PROVISIONAL OPERATION USEFUL DURING CERTAIN PROGRAM DEVELOPMENT.  FOR MOST
/	USERS THE DEFAULT VALUES SHOULD BE PRESERVED AS STATED HERE.  ONLY THOSE
/	QUALIFIED TO MAKE INFORMED CHANGES SHOULD VENTURE FORTH IN THIS SECTION.

	IFNDEF	MACHINE	<MACHINE=0>	/THE DEFAULT IS THE 8K VERSION; CHANGE TO
					/"MACHINE=1" TO USE THE LAB-8/E PERIPHERALS.

	IFNDEF	DEB4K	<DEB4K=	0>	/THE DEFAULT IS NO DEBUGGING IN FIELD 0; BINARY
					/COMPATIBILITY WILL BE MAINTAINED.  CHANGE TO
					/"DEB4K=1" TO USE BREAKPOINTS IN FIELD 0.  FOR
					/USE ONLY WITH CODE NOT SUBJECT TO INTERRUPTS.

	IFNDEF	DEB8K	<DEB8K=	0>	/THE DEFAULT IS NO DEBUGGING IN THE EXTENDED
					/MEMORY FIELD.  CHANGE TO 1 TO ALLOW BREAKPOINTS
					/IN THE EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD.  USE ONLY WITH
					/DEB4K ALSO SET TO 1, AS MANY DEBUGGERS WILL
					/ALSO MODIFY LOCATION 00004 REGARDLESS OF FIELD
					/IN WHICH THE ACTUAL BREAKPOINT IS LOCATED IN.

	IFNDEF	MFLD	<MFLD=	0>	/THE DEFAULT IS TO USE FIELD 0 AS THE MAIN CODE
					/LOADING FIELD.  CHANGE AS NECESSARY ACCORDING
					/TO DOCUMENTATION REGARDING TESTING BINARY
					/OUTPUT FOR SOURCE CODE ACCURACY.  ALL OTHER
					/SETTINGS WILL PRODUCE A NONFUNCTIONAL BINARY
					/OUTPUT, ETC.

	IFNDEF	XFLD	<XFLD=	1>	/THE DEFAULT IS TO USE FIELD 1 AS THE EXTENDED
					/MEMORY LOADING FIELD.  CHANGE AS NECESSARY
					/ACCORDING TO DOCUMENTATION ABOUT PROGRAM
					/LOADING AND/OR TESTING, ETC.

	IFNDEF	XEQFL0	<XEQFL0=0>	/THE CODE IN FIELD 0 ASSEMBLES FOR FIELD 0
					/REGARDLESS OF WHERE THE CODE IS LOADED.  IF
					/THIS VALUE IS CHANGED THE BINARY OUTPUT WILL BE
					/NON-FUNCTIONAL BECAUSE OF THE HARD-WIRED NATURE
					/OF INTERRUPTS TO FIELD 0, ETC.

	IFNDEF	XEQFL1	<XEQFL1=1>	/THE CODE IN FIELD 1 ASSEMBLES FOR FIELD 1
					/REGARDLESS OF WHERE THE CODE IS LOADED.  THIS
					/VALUE SHOULD BE CHANGED TO MATCH THE SETTING OF
					/"XFLD" TO PRODUCE A WORKING VERSION INTENDED TO
					/LOAD INTO A DIFFERENT EXTENDED MEMORY FIELD.
					/DO NOT CHANGE "XEQFL1" WHEN CHANGING "XFLD" IF
					/TESTING BINARY OUTPUT FOR SOURCE CODE ACCURACY
					/AS IN THAT INSTANCE THE LOADING FIELD DIFFERS
					/FROM THE EXECUTION FIELD, ETC.
/	A PARAMETER MAY BE ADDED TO EXPLOIT LOCATION 00004 FOR DEBUGGING PURPOSES.  AS
/	WRITTEN, LAB-8/E BASIC OR 8K BASIC IS INCOMPATIBLE WITH VIRTUALLY ALL SOFTWARE
/	DEBUGGERS FOR THE PDP-8.  SOME OPTIMIZATION COULD FREE THIS LOCATION BY
/	INTRODUCING A SMALL AMOUNT OF BINARY INCOMPATIBILITY.  THE STANDARD VERSION
/	WILL NOT BE COMPATIBLE WITH THE ABILITY TO DEBUG WHILE MAINTAINING 100% BINARY
/	COMPATIBILITY WITH THE ORIGINAL RELEASE.

/	ADDITIONAL GLOBAL SETTINGS WILL BE ADDED AS NECESSARY.

/	BY SETTING "MFLD=2" AND "XFLD=3", THE SOURCE CODE CAN BE PROVEN TO BE CONSISTENT
/	WITH THE ORIGINAL RELEASED BINARY PAPER-TAPE.  "XEQFL0=0" AND "XEQFL1=1" MUST
/	ALSO BE SET TO PRESERVE THE ACTUAL CODE FOR THE COMPARISON.  THE COMPARISON
/	ITSELF IS CARRIED OUT USING A COMPARE PROGRAM LOADED INTO ANOTHER UNRELATED
/	MEMORY FIELD ALONG WITH PRE-ZEROING ALL RELEVANT MEMORY LOCATIONS IN ALL FOUR
/	FIELDS LOADED:

/	FIELD 0 CONTAINS THE BINARY FROM THE RELEASED BINARY PAPER TAPE OF 8K BASIC.

/	FIELD 1 CONTAINS THE BINARY FROM THE RELEASED BINARY PAPER TAPE OF 8K BASIC.

/	FIELD 2 CONTAINS THE FIELD 0 BINARY CREATED BY ASSEMBLY OF THIS SOURCE FILE.

/	FIELD 3 CONTAINS THE FIELD 1 BINARY CREATED BY ASSEMBLY OF THIS SOURCE FILE.

/	FIELD 4 [OR HIGHER] SHOULD CONTAIN THE ACTUAL COMPARISON PROGRAM.  IF ALL
/	LOCATIONS IN FIELDS 0 AND 1 VERSUS FIELDS 2 AND 3 COMPARE EQUALLY [DISREGARDING
/	7600-7777 IN EVERY FIELD] THIS PROVES THE SOURCE CODE IS INTACT.  NOTE: CERTAIN
/	CONDITIONAL MODIFICATIONS MUST BE DISABLED DURING THIS TEST PHASE!

/	OS/8 OR SUITABLY CONFIGURED P?S/8 SYSTEMS CAN BE USED TO LOAD THIS TEST
/	CONFIGURATION.  NO LOADED LOCATION VIOLATES THE MEMORY CONSTRAINTS OF EITHER
/	SYSTEM.  HOWEVER, THE PROGRAM CANNOT BE RUN IN ORIGINAL FORM FROM OS/8 BEYOND
/	THE LOADING FOR TEST PURPOSES.  ALL VERSIONS THAT CHANGE THE OPERATIONAL FIELD
/	PARAMETERS FOR THE FIELD 0 PORTION WILL PRODUCE A NON-FUNCTIONAL RESULT NOT
/	SUITABLE FOR ANY PURPOSE BEYOND COMPARISON TESTING.

	MFLD=	2			/MAIN CODE LOADED INTO FIELD 2

	XFLD=	3			/EXTENDED MEMORY CODE LOADED INTO FIELD 3

	XEQFL0=	0			/FIELD 0 CODE ASSEMBLED FOR USE IN FIELD 0

	XEQFL1=	1			/FIELD 1 CODE ASSEMBLED FOR USE IN FIELD 1
/	THE FOLLOWING CODE IS USED IN THE LAB-8/E BASIC VERSION TO MAKE THE PROGRAM
/	SELF-STARTING.  PAPER-TAPE CHECKSUMMING IS PROPERLY OBSERVED.  [NOTE: THIS
/	CODE ASSUMES THE USUAL VERSION OF THE DEC BIN LOADER IS PERFORMING THE
/	PAPER-TAPE LOADING.]

	IFNZRO	MACHINE			<

	FIELD	XFLD			/THIS IS WHERE THE BIN LOADER SHOULD BE LOADED

	*7700				/OVER THE BINARY LOADER CODE EXIT ROUTINE

	JMP I	UGETWD/(6000)		/JUST GO START OUR PROGRAM DIRECTLY

	*6000				/ONCE-ONLY CODE CAN RUN FROM HERE

	DCA	UTEMP			/SAVE THE CHECKSUM
	TAD	INTHLT/(HLT)		/GET A HLT INSTRUCTION
	DCA I	INTPLC/(7700)		/RESTORE THE BINARY LOADER TO NORMAL
	TAD	UTEMP			/GET THE CHECKSUM
	SZA				/SKIP IF LOAD OK
	JMP I	INTPLC			/GOTO NORMAL HLT LOCATION WITH BAD CHECKSUM
	XEQFL0^10+CIF CDF		/WE STARTUP IN FIELD 0
	JMP I	.+1/(START)		/GO START IT UP
	START				/THROUGH HERE

INTHLT,	HLT				/CONSTANT 7402
INTPLC,	7700				/ADDRESS OF OVERLAYED BIN LOADER CODE

					>

/	COMMENTARY BY CHARLES LASNER.

/	THERE DOESN'T SEEM TO BE A COMPARABLE ROUTINE FOR AUTOMATIC SELF-START OF 8K
/	BASIC. IF THE BIN LOADER IS ACTIVELY IN FIELD 0 LOADING THE PROGRAM, THERE
/	DOES NOT SEEM TO BE A REASON NOT TO OVERLAY THE LOADER IN AN ANALOGOUS FASHION
/	TO ACHIEVE THE EQUIVALENT MINOR FRILL.

/	THE AREA OF MEMORY IN FIELD 1 POSSIBLY OCCUPIED BY THE BIN LOADER WILL BE
/	DESTROYED BY STARTING LAB-8/E OR 8K BASIC.  IF THE RIM LOADER IS STILL PRESENT
/	IN FIELD 1 IT CAN BE USED TO RESTORE MEMORY WITHOUT TOGGLING IN ANY ADDITIONAL
/	CODE.  THIS COULD ALSO BE USEFUL IF LAB-8/E OR 8K BASIC IS MODIFIED BY THE
/	USER TO IMPLEMENT THE USER-DEFINED FUNCTION.

/	IT WOULD BE USEFUL TO MAKE THESE LOADING CONSIDERATIONS CLEARER IN SOME FUTURE
/	SOURCE FILE RELEASE IN A DOCUMENTATION SECTION.  HOWEVER, THE SOURCE CODE WAS
/	NOT MODIFIED TO PRESERVE THE ORIGINAL INTENT OF THE AUTHORS.
/	BASIC4.52

